From: mscharged-port
Subject: Compile pipelines that recorded draws miss in parallel
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4

A draw whose pipeline was not compiled yet waited on the GX FIFO worker until
the single pipeline thread had compiled it synchronously, so the pipelines a
new effect needs in one frame compiled one after another (in one recorded
match, 25 in a single frame took 220 ms, the slowest 16 ms) and the rest of
the frame could not be recorded meanwhile.

Recorded draws now use a Demand priority: the compile starts at once with
CreateRenderPipelineAsync on Dawn's worker pool, without the preparation
limit, and recording continues. get_pipeline waits for a pipeline that is
still being compiled, so the render worker waits when it encodes the draw:
the draw keeps its FIFO position and is never dropped. A failed asynchronous
creation is retried synchronously, as for preparation. Without a pipeline
thread, draws still compile inline.

The demand miss report (0108) follows the compile: a missed draw registers
one report per pipeline, made before the pipeline is published, with the time
from the draw's request until it was ready (compileNanoseconds; pipelines
missed together overlap). Reports therefore run on the thread that published
the pipeline. demandWaitNanoseconds now sums how long encoders waited, the
actual stall. With 25 recorded configurations missed at once on a busy 4-core
laptop (driver cache off), the wait fell from 0.85-1.04 s one after another
to 0.23-0.34 s (four runs each).

